 true] [17592186381061 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536050 true] [17592186381061 1041 "selection" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381061 1104 #edb/ref 17592186381065 13194139536050 true] [17592186381061 1110 #uuid "82f290ef-7181-5939-98e5-4ec370b604cd" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381062 1034 "2\\sqrt{3}" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381062 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381062 1111 #uuid "677a1443-b485-5c21-b2a3-d43bfbc7873b" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381063 1034 "3" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381063 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381063 1111 #uuid "0157603f-97d1-5f78-8203-0f605d82e42f" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381064 1034 "4" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381064 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381064 1111 #uuid "d469c7cf-3cc5-54a2-a4f3-b4092494d8b3" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381065 1034 "5" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381065 1111 #uuid "7ce13ade-1ae9-5f03-9514-280bb05a76e5" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381066 1034 "5\\sqrt{2}" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381066 1111 #uuid "bc916ddc-00ed-5718-96a3-2ea085324613" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381067 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381068 13194139536050 true] [17592186381067 1089 "q-177950" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381067 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536050 true] [17592186381067 1100 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381067 1113 #uuid "96d393f1-651b-502e-b281-70311ebb53c8" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381067 1145 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381069 13194139536050 true] [17592186381068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381070 13194139536050 true] [17592186381068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381071 13194139536050 true] [17592186381068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381072 13194139536050 true] [17592186381068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381073 13194139536050 true] [17592186381068 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536050 true] [17592186381068 1041 "selection" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381068 1104 #edb/ref 17592186381072 13194139536050 true] [17592186381068 1110 #uuid "89e69491-e242-5d53-a5d6-e44b49980bdf" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381069 1034 "-8" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381069 1111 #uuid "80954421-08eb-5a6e-8863-e0803e76872a" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381070 1034 "8" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381070 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381070 1111 #uuid "8080f578-ff72-5244-94f8-3e740a493c65" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381071 1034 "2" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381071 1111 #uuid "554920c6-385e-532f-9b99-a1167d813b0e" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381072 1034 "-4" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381072 1111 #uuid "5b3dc04c-bd8f-5561-be3b-0ed284e34c29" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381073 1034 "4" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536050 true] [17592186381073 1111 #uuid "895a6e32-f6c1-5f0a-8c51-c9720068b325" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381074 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381075 13194139536050 true] [17592186381074 1089 "q-178026" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381074 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536050 true] [17592186381074 1100 "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381074 1113 #uuid "28a2a0df-bfc0-5db2-9bfc-e0ca753a94b4" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381074 1145 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{12}{25}+\\frac{12}{25}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $Q({u}_{1})$ and $Q({u}_{2})$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}+(\\frac{3}{5})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(-\\frac{3}{5})^{2}+(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381075 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381076 13194139536050 true] [17592186381075 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381077 13194139536050 true] [17592186381075 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381078 13194139536050 true] [17592186381075 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381079 13194139536050 true] [17592186381075 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381080 13194139536050 true] [17592186381075 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536050 true] [17592186381075 1041 "selection" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381075 1104 #edb/ref 17592186381076 13194139536050 true] [17592186381075 1110 #uuid "26eea3b0-6446-5336-8020-5c9c19031678" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381076 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536050 true] [17592186381076 1111 #uuid "6e38975e-af87-59f9-8e4e-c0833f00aa76" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381077 1034 "I only" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381077 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536050 true] [17592186381077 1111 #uuid "6b17c4cd-e3fb-5f4b-be2d-49e57d195bcc" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381078 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381078 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536050 true] [17592186381078 1111 #uuid "607e30cd-6e62-566b-9e4e-4ad9dc5bdcdc" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381079 1034 "None of the statements are true." 13194139536050 true] [17592186381079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536050 true] [17592186381079 1111 #uuid "c7153c37-a2f8-5447-8b1b-e9b7e00bacd5" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381080 1034 "II only" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536050 true] [17592186381080 1111 #uuid "9d39ebd7-7e7a-5380-aded-f9bd5fbd7aee" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381081 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381082 13194139536050 true] [17592186381081 1089 "q-178269" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381081 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536050 true] [17592186381081 1100 "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381081 1113 #uuid "594006d7-2a08-5090-b56a-13bc6c29a3d7" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381081 1145 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=6+3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139536050 true] [17592186381082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381083 13194139536050 true] [17592186381082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381084 13194139536050 true] [17592186381082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381085 13194139536050 true] [17592186381082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381086 13194139536050 true] [17592186381082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381087 13194139536050 true] [17592186381082 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045